        "title": "Explore Unfold in a classifier",
        "subtitle": "Read small rectangles from parallel grids into ordered columns.",
        "disclosureTitle": "How patch positions and overlaps work",
        "formula": "Read small rectangles from parallel grids into ordered columns.",
        "paragraphs": [
          "An array is a table of numbers extended across named directions. Each observation contains channels, meaning parallel grids, with height and width. A batch is a group of independent observations. A patch is a small rectangle read from each grid.",
          "Patch size chooses rectangle height and width. Spacing between patches moves each starting position; Spacing within a patch separates the positions read inside it. Zero padding adds a border of zeros, not extra measured values. Every control is a height/width pair.",
          "The output is a table of columns. Each column represents one patch position, ordered top to bottom and left to right. Rows contain channel values, then positions within a patch in row/column order. Overlap repeats some original measurements in several columns.",
          "Input influence means how a small source change affects a result. Repeated measurements add their influences; zero-padding entries contribute none. Two lessons compare ordinary overlaps and padded, spaced patches; identical output sizes need not imply identical values."
        ],
        "buttonTitle": "Open Unfold example",
        "exampleSource": "Sources/KwipMLCore/SpatialPatchWorkflowExample.swift",
        "lessonIDs": [
          "assessment.unfold-overlapping-windows",
          "assessment.unfold-padded-spaced-windows"
        ]
      }
    },
