        "sourceResource": "workflow-observations",
        "title": "Explore Fold in a classifier",
        "subtitle": "Add ordered rectangle contributions back into parallel grids.",
        "disclosureTitle": "How patch positions and overlaps work",
        "formula": "Add ordered rectangle contributions back into parallel grids.",
        "paragraphs": [
          "An array is a table of numbers with named directions. Each observation begins with packed patch rows and patch-position columns. A batch groups independent observations. A patch is a small rectangle; channels are parallel grids.",
          "Output grid size chooses the destination height and width. Patch size, Spacing between patches, Spacing within a patch and Zero padding determine where the supplied contributions belong. Packed rows must divide exactly by patch area, and the number of columns must match the output geometry.",
          "When rectangles overlap, their contributions are added. Fold never averages or automatically divides by overlap counts, so it is not generally an inverse of Unfold. Grid positions that no patch covers stay zero. Entries belonging only to padding are ignored.",
          "Input influence means how a small contribution changes the final grid. The measured lessons check overlapping addition and uncovered positions, including ignored border entries. Inspect the grid output rather than assuming it reproduces an earlier source."
        ],
        "buttonTitle": "Open Fold example",
        "exampleSource": "Sources/KwipMLCore/SpatialPatchWorkflowExample.swift",
